WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:08.880
On the 26th of August 1346, south of the&nbsp;
port town of Calais in northern France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:16.640
an outnumbered English army commanded by King&nbsp;
Edward III of England confronted the forces of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:26.160
King Philip VI of France. As the fighting raged,&nbsp;
the English right flank was furiously assaulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:33.920
by the French. Despite the immense odds and after&nbsp;
being thrown to the ground and nearly losing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:41.840
life in the fighting, it was the 16-year old son&nbsp;
of the English king, Prince Edward, who rallied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:49.920
their forces and beat back the French. Victory at&nbsp;
the Battle of Crécy was only the beginning of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:57.920
remarkable career of Prince Edward. Who was he,&nbsp;
how did he contribute to the Hundred Years’ War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:14.000
and why did he never become King of England? This&nbsp;
is the story of Edward, the Black Prince. 
&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:29.280
The man known to history as Edward of Woodstock,&nbsp;
or Edward the Black Prince, was born on the 15th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:38.160
of June 1330 at Woodstock Palace in Oxfordshire&nbsp;
in England. The origins of the name ‘The Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:46.240
Prince’ are not known for certain. Prince Edward&nbsp;
was not called by this name when he was alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:54.320
Rather it was adopted during the Tudor era, over&nbsp;
a hundred years after his death. Some speculate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:00.880
that the name arises from the black armour he&nbsp;
was said to have habitually worn into battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:07.680
whilst others claim that it was derived from&nbsp;
the dark reputation he garnered by engaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:15.840
in scorched earth campaigns or chevauchées&nbsp;
across France during the Hundred Years’ War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:23.200
A third theory is that this name comes from&nbsp;
his coat of arms, which depicted three ostrich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:33.280
feathers set against a black background. 
The Black Prince’s father was King Edward III of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:42.320
England, the son and heir of King Edward II.&nbsp;
Edward III became king in 1327 at the age of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:51.600
fourteen after his weak and unpopular father was&nbsp;
deposed by his wife Isabella and her lover, Roger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:59.840
Mortimer, a powerful English noble who along with&nbsp;
Isabella organised an uprising while in France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:09.760
and invaded England in 1326, rapidly supplanting&nbsp;
Edward’s government. Edward II’s great mistake was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:17.440
to grant excessive power throughout his reign to a&nbsp;
series of court favourites such as Piers Gaveston&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:27.360
and the Despenser family, actions which ultimately&nbsp;
cost him his throne and his life in 1327. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:35.680
Black Prince’s mother was Phillipa of Hainault,&nbsp;
the daughter of William I, Count of Hainault. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:43.200
was Queen of England from the date of her marriage&nbsp;
to Edward III on the 24th of January 1328 until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:53.760
her death on the 15th of August 1369. Edward and&nbsp;
Phillipa would ultimately have thirteen children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:04:03.600
of whom Prince Edward was the eldest.
When Edward was born in the summer of 1330,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:10.320
his father was still basically a puppet of&nbsp;
his mother and Mortimer. They had managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:19.040
overthrow the unpopular Edward II in 1327 and then&nbsp;
seized control of England, with Isabella’s teenage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:28.320
son being king in name only at first. However,&nbsp;
Edward III soon showed that he was nothing like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:35.040
his father in terms of his political and military&nbsp;
abilities. It was the birth of his son and heir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:42.080
Edward of Woodstock, that galvanised the King to&nbsp;
move against the increasingly unpopular Mortimer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:50.960
and Isabella. On the 19th of October 1330, Edward&nbsp;
III raided a castle where the couple were residing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:05:00.320
in Nottingham and captured them. On the 29th of&nbsp;
November Mortimer was executed, while Isabella was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:08.320
placed under house confinement and banned from all&nbsp;
political activity. Edward’s decisive reassertion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:15.280
of royal power was welcomed by the English&nbsp;
nobility. The young king immediately resolved to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:22.640
wage war on the Scots to avenge the humiliation of&nbsp;
the Battle of Bannockburn in 1314, through which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:32.400
Edward II had lost control over Scotland. Edward&nbsp;
III’s well-trained army secured a complete victory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:41.680
over the Scottish forces at the Battle of Halidon&nbsp;
Hill on the 19th of July 1333. Edward was received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:49.600
as a hero on his return to England. Thereafter&nbsp;
he was determined to rebuild England’s might as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:55.680
it had existed in the time of his grandfather,&nbsp;
King Edward I, or indeed in the days of King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:02.960
Henry II in the twelfth century when the English&nbsp;
king had ruled over most of northern and western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:15.600
France as well. In this task of royal renewal, his&nbsp;
eldest son would become an able participant. 
&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:23.120
Details of the early life of Edward of Woodstock&nbsp;
are scarce. He was made the Earl of Chester in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:30.720
1333, his first title, and was brought up in&nbsp;
the surroundings of a magnificent court, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:38.880
feasting and celebration were abundant. The young&nbsp;
prince was dressed in fine silk clothes and given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:46.480
a personal tailor. However, the prince’s father&nbsp;
also wanted to ensure that his heir could perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:54.560
his duties as a military ruler as well one day.&nbsp;
To this end, the Prince received his first suit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.560 --> 00:07:02.640
of armour at the age of 7, and from the age of 13&nbsp;
participated in the numerous chivalric tournaments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:10.400
held by the King. The first decade of his reign&nbsp;
saw Edward III embroiled in further conflict with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:17.280
the Scottish King, David II, leading multiple&nbsp;
campaigns in the Scottish borderlands following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:26.640
Halidon Hill. However, his thoughts soon turned&nbsp;
towards England’s greatest enemy, France, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:35.280
in 1337 Edward launched what would become known&nbsp;
as the Hundred Years’ War, the conflict in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:42.480
the Black Prince would establish himself as one of&nbsp;
the great military leaders of the medieval period.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:50.320
Edward’s claim to the French throne originated&nbsp;
from the death in 1328 of King Charles IV of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:59.040
France without a male heir, leaving two chief&nbsp;
claimants for the throne: Philip, Count of Valois,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:07.040
Edward’s cousin, and Edward himself, whose&nbsp;
mother Isabella was Charles’ sister. The French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:15.120
aristocracy chose Philip of Valois to become&nbsp;
Philip VI of France. This choice was not contested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:21.840
at first, primarily because at the time Isabella&nbsp;
and Mortimer wanted to maintain good relations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:29.040
with the French. Indeed, even when Edward&nbsp;
took personal control of England late in 1330,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:35.440
he maintained cordial relations with Philip&nbsp;
as he dealt first with the Scots in the years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:44.880
that followed. But relations between the English&nbsp;
and French monarchs soured gradually and in 1337&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:51.280
England went to war with France after Philip&nbsp;
threatened to confiscate the Duchy of Gascony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:59.280
England’s last major holding on the continent.&nbsp;
At first Edward went to war exclusively over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:07.120
the Gascony issue, but early in 1340 Edward&nbsp;
extended the cause for war by publicly asserting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:15.840
his claim to the French crown. Thus, the war&nbsp;
that would rage on and off down to 1453 was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:22.000
over England’s right to the French throne,&nbsp;
but also the effort to reclaim territories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:29.440
like the Duchy of Normandy that England had&nbsp;
lost back in the early thirteenth century. 	
&nbsp;

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:38.960
Edward’s initial plan in the war was to draw&nbsp;
the French forces into a decisive battle. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:46.880
he invaded northern France in the autumn of 1339,&nbsp;
using scorched earth tactics to devastate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:54.640
countryside. However, King Philip refused to meet&nbsp;
the English in open battle and withdrew his forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.640 --> 00:10:02.880
into defensive positions, forcing Edward back&nbsp;
to Flanders without a victory. It was there that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:11.040
he formally declared his claim to the French&nbsp;
throne in January 1340 in the city of Ghent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:18.400
By then Edward was desperate for a victory over&nbsp;
the French, as heavy taxation at home in England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:26.480
to pay for his army was unpopular. He therefore&nbsp;
assembled more than 100 ships at Holbrook in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:34.560
East of England to ferry his troops to Flanders&nbsp;
and began a new offensive with his Flemish allies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:41.360
Yet the French had anticipated this move and&nbsp;
were blockading his entry into Flanders with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:50.160
a fleet twice as large as his own. Edward received&nbsp;
news of this, but nonetheless chose to sail on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:59.120
22nd of June. The fleets clashed two days later&nbsp;
near the town of Sluys. Though the French boats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:06.480
were faster, their sailors were inexperienced in&nbsp;
combat, while Edward’s vessels were crammed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:13.760
skilled archers. Furthermore, the French boats&nbsp;
were bound together to maintain a defensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:21.840
position, making the fleet extremely immobile.&nbsp;
The English consequently won a total victory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:30.320
capturing almost the entire French fleet. Despite&nbsp;
the stunning naval success at the Battle of Sluys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:35.520
Edward could still not force King&nbsp;
Philip into a decisive battle on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:45.760
French territory. He soon ran out of funds&nbsp;
and was forced to abandon his campaign. 
&nbsp;

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:54.880
After peace talks in 1344 ended in failure,&nbsp;
Edward launched a major new campaign in 1346.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:01.600
By now the Black Prince was 16 years old and&nbsp;
considered to be old enough to take part in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:10.320
the war. He had also been made the Prince of&nbsp;
Wales in 1343. Father and son, with their army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:19.680
landed in Normandy on the 12th of July 1346 with&nbsp;
a 14,000-strong army. Upon their arrival there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:26.400
the Prince was knighted by his father and&nbsp;
given command of the vanguard of the army.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:32.880
After pillaging the town of Caen in late July,&nbsp;
with the Black Prince playing a leading role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:41.120
the town’s capture, Edward’s army continued its&nbsp;
easterly march through Normandy towards Calais.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:47.760
After crossing the River Somme on the 24th of&nbsp;
August they arrived on a ridge of high ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:56.880
between the villages of Crecy and Wadicourt.&nbsp;
The English king had around 2,500 men-at-arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:06.800
3,000 cavalry, 2,000 spearmen and 5,000 to 7,000&nbsp;
longbowmen, whilst a swiftly approaching French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:15.520
force was much larger, numbering over 20,000 men.&nbsp;
However, the English were well-rested when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:23.680
battle commenced on the 26th, whereas the French&nbsp;
were tired after a long march northwards. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:29.760
King Edward commanded the centre of the army&nbsp;
and the Earl of Northampton the left, the Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:37.200
Prince was given command of the right wing. The&nbsp;
French advance was led by Genoese crossbowmen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:44.240
with the extensive French cavalry building up&nbsp;
behind them. Seeing the Genoese falter under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:50.560
heavy English longbow fire, they charged&nbsp;
through the crossbowmen, causing chaos and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:57.440
confusion in the French ranks. While this slowed&nbsp;
the French onslaught, in the early evening the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:03.920
main French force arrived on the battlefield&nbsp;
and charged the English right under the Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:11.840
Prince. This led to a severe struggle, in which&nbsp;
the Prince himself was beaten to the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:18.560
However, reinforcements from the English left&nbsp;
helped repel the French attack, and Prince Edward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:27.120
though badly concussed, soon picked himself up and&nbsp;
continued fighting valiantly. As daylight faded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:35.040
the French launched wave after wave of cavalry&nbsp;
attacks to no avail. King Philip himself had two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:42.800
horses slain under him during the battle and&nbsp;
received an arrow wound in his face. As night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:50.000
fell, with his army disintegrating around&nbsp;
him against a steady English advance, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:59.440
reluctantly fled the field, conceding a terrible&nbsp;
defeat to King Edward’s triumphant army. 
&nbsp;

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:06.320
The Battle of Crecy was one of the greatest&nbsp;
English victories of the Hundred Years’ War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:12.880
with Edward’s army sustaining fewer than 300&nbsp;
casualties, while the French casualties were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:19.600
far higher, including a great number of prominent&nbsp;
nobles, such as Philip’s brother, the Count of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:27.120
Alencon and King John of Bohemia. The Black Prince&nbsp;
and his father came across the body of King John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:35.280
when surveying the battlefield the following day.&nbsp;
They were deeply moved, for John was one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:43.200
foremost warriors of his age, losing his sight&nbsp;
whilst on crusade. It is said that the Prince took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:50.720
John’s emblem of three white feathers and made it&nbsp;
his own. John’s emblem remains the symbol of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:58.800
Prince of Wales to this day. Following the battle,&nbsp;
Edward laid siege to the port of Calais, whilst an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:05.920
invading Scottish army, encouraged by Philip to&nbsp;
exploit Edward’s absence, was decisively defeated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:14.880
at Neville’s Cross by an English force under the&nbsp;
Archbishop of York on the 17th of October 1346.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:24.480
Calais finally surrendered in August 1347 and&nbsp;
in October of that year, King Edward negotiated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:33.360
a truce and returned to London. 
The spirit of jubilation across England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:41.680
surrounding Edward and his son’s great victories&nbsp;
was quickly brought to an end, when in August 1348&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:49.200
the Black Death crossed the English Channel&nbsp;
and ravaged the country. The bubonic plague&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:57.680
would eventually kill over one-third of Europe’s&nbsp;
population. Densely populated cities like London&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:04.480
were particularly badly hit. Indeed, the Black&nbsp;
Prince was personally impacted by the disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:12.480
as it claimed the life of his sister Joan on&nbsp;
the 2nd of September 1348. Nevertheless, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:20.160
spite of the plague, King Edward inaugurated a new&nbsp;
chivalric order at Windsor Castle that same year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:27.760
naming it the Order of the Garter. It had 26&nbsp;
original members including the Black Prince,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:34.240
who had assisted his father in selecting the&nbsp;
other founding members, all of whom were selected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:42.160
based on acts of valour, rather than simply&nbsp;
their rank. The Order was arguably the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:49.440
enduring legacy of King Edward III’s reign, as&nbsp;
it remains the most senior order of knights in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:58.240
the United Kingdom to this day. As the effects of&nbsp;
the plague began to dissipate in late 1349, King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:05.360
Edward saw the chance to reclaim the offensive&nbsp;
against France. After hearing of an imminent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:12.400
attempt to recapture Calais, the king and his&nbsp;
son went to the vital port ahead of the French,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:19.520
and sprung a trap on their enemy, repulsing&nbsp;
the French force on the 1st of January 1350,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:26.800
although not without the Black Prince having to&nbsp;
lead his garrison in saving his father, after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:36.880
Edward became stranded outside the city gates,&nbsp;
against a much larger enemy force. 
&nbsp;

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:43.040
The Black Prince owned large estates&nbsp;
throughout England. He implemented a host&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:50.640
of revenue-generating policies when his income&nbsp;
fell after the Black Death. During the crisis he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:58.640
showed himself to be a fair landlord, instructing&nbsp;
his revenue official in Cornwall in 1351, to ‘deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:06.080
equitably and rightly’ with his tenants and to&nbsp;
protect them from ‘oppressions and extortions’.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:13.440
Furthermore, after local unrest in Cheshire&nbsp;
in the summer of 1353, the Prince travelled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:20.160
to the county himself, holding judicial&nbsp;
sessions to settle grievances. He also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:28.320
agreed to grant Cheshire a charter of liberties.&nbsp;
This successful management of his lands aside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:34.400
the Black Prince was also gaining a&nbsp;
reputation as an overly indulgent spender,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:42.480
constantly living beyond his financial means to&nbsp;
fund his extravagant lifestyle. This being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:49.760
it should be noted that the Prince’s extravagance&nbsp;
was not mindless spending. In medieval England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:56.720
great lords needed to be generous in feasting and&nbsp;
gift-giving to recruit men into their service and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.720 --> 00:20:03.600
maintain their allegiance. Striking a balance&nbsp;
between rewarding one’s followers and avoiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:10.800
the burden of debt was a challenge faced by many&nbsp;
medieval lords, especially after the Black Death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:16.720
when there were labour shortages all across&nbsp;
the continent and soldiers and farmers were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:25.840
in an excellent negotiating position when it&nbsp;
came to the retention of their services. 
&nbsp;

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:33.120
In 1355, Jean de Grailly, a knight of the&nbsp;
garter from Gascony, the English-held province&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:38.880
in south-western France, informed King Edward&nbsp;
that people in the region were concerned with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:46.240
the actions of the Count of Armagnac, who had been&nbsp;
appointed by King John II of France as governor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:53.760
of the southern region of Languedoc, and who had&nbsp;
captured a number of Gascon towns and fortresses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.320 --> 00:21:00.960
Edward was keen to resume the offensive against&nbsp;
France and his Great Council decided that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:07.520
Black Prince should lead an expedition from&nbsp;
Gascony to secure the province and punish the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:14.400
Count of Armagnac. Furthermore, on the 10th of&nbsp;
July the Prince was made the King’s lieutenant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:22.160
in Gascony, allowing him to basically govern&nbsp;
Gascony as a de-facto royal viceroy. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:30.640
given an army of 2,600 men, to be bolstered by&nbsp;
Gascon soldiers. Accompanying the Prince to France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:37.840
were fourteen knights of the Garter, including the&nbsp;
experienced Earls of Warwick and Suffolk and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:45.120
Prince’s special advisers and close friends,&nbsp;
Sir John Chandos and Sir James Audley. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:52.960
reached Bordeaux on the 20th of September 1355.&nbsp;
The following day a religious ceremony was held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.960 --> 00:22:00.160
in Saint-Andre Cathedral, after which the Prince&nbsp;
spoke directly to the inhabitants of Bordeaux,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:06.560
telling them of his plan to take the offensive&nbsp;
against the Languedoc governor, by marching east&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:14.240
and ravaging his lands in a great chevauchée,&nbsp;
a French term meaning a brutal plundering raid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:21.840
where land either side of the advancing column&nbsp;
was burned. The aim of a chevauchée was to plunder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:28.080
the wealth and resources of the enemy’s land in&nbsp;
order to undermine the enemy’s capacity to wage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:36.800
war. With the Gascon contingents joining his army,&nbsp;
the Black Prince’s force reached around 8,000 men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:43.520
and on the 5th October they set off from Bordeaux&nbsp;
following the river Garonne, and entering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:51.760
Armagnac’s lands on the 12th of October, where&nbsp;
the troops began burning, and fires soon raged for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:59.760
many miles around the army. Though Armagnac was in&nbsp;
command of a larger force than the Anglo-Gascons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:06.480
he did not want to risk battle against the&nbsp;
Black Prince’s experienced men. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:12.160
he gathered his army in the fortified city of&nbsp;
Toulouse and destroyed the bridges over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:23.280
Garonne and Ariege rivers, hoping the raid would&nbsp;
fizzle out or come to a halt before the rivers. 
&nbsp;

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:30.320
On the 28th of October the Prince reached the&nbsp;
Garonne south of Toulouse. The Prince had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:37.600
informed by local guides in his service that&nbsp;
the stream could be forded. As such, he led his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:46.320
army across the river. This audacious move greatly&nbsp;
boosted the morale of his men, while causing panic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:54.000
amongst Armagnac and the French, who assumed the&nbsp;
Prince would turn back before the rivers. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.000 --> 00:24:01.280
his army now marched east through the Languedoc,&nbsp;
a region totally unprepared for the carnage that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.280 --> 00:24:07.680
bore down upon them. The army reached the&nbsp;
major town of Carcassonne and razed its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:14.320
outer settlements to the ground on the 6th of&nbsp;
November, and then continued on to Narbonne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:22.080
which was occupied on the 8th of November. The&nbsp;
Prince’s troops had now covered 250 miles since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:29.840
leaving Bordeaux and were within sight of the&nbsp;
Mediterranean Sea. Edward ultimately decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:36.560
return to Gascony before the winter weather set&nbsp;
in. The Prince’s army re-crossed the Garonne on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:43.120
the 20th of November, and after receiving&nbsp;
reinforcements, the Count of Armagnac finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:51.440
left Toulouse. He shadowed the Anglo-Gascons as&nbsp;
they marched west, much to the fury of John II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:58.000
the French King. On the 28th of November,&nbsp;
the Prince arrived back on Gascon soil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:04.560
and spent Christmas at Bordeaux amid great&nbsp;
rejoicing and celebration, for the expedition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:13.280
had been a clear success. Many Gascon lords who&nbsp;
had joined the French, now rallied to the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:23.840
cause, as Armagnac’s failure to protect his&nbsp;
vassal’s lands, had been brutally exposed. 
	
&nbsp;

00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:32.480
The chevauchée of 1355 further demonstrated the&nbsp;
Black Prince’s military skill. As news of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:39.280
success arrived to England, Edward III sensed&nbsp;
an opportunity to launch a wider offensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:47.360
against his enemy, as King John II was facing a&nbsp;
crisis in his relations with the French nobility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:54.400
originating from John’s arrest of the powerful&nbsp;
aristocrat Charles of Navarre and other Norman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.400 --> 00:26:02.880
lords at a dinner party in Rouen, when he threw&nbsp;
Charles in jail and executed four nobles, causing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:10.640
Normandy to rise in revolt against the French&nbsp;
King. To exploit this situation, King Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:18.080
sent a small force under the Duke of Lancaster to&nbsp;
Normandy at the beginning of June 1356 and urged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:25.120
the Black Prince to march north to link up with&nbsp;
Lancaster’s army, in order to attack John II’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:33.360
son, the Count of Poitiers, who was charged with&nbsp;
the defence of France south of the Loire River.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:39.840
The Prince was delayed in his expedition by a&nbsp;
resurgent Count of Armagnac, whose incursions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:48.000
into Gascony forced the Prince to leave 2,000 of&nbsp;
his 8,000-strong force in the duchy to defend it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:54.960
before he began his march on the 4th of August,&nbsp;
moving north through France and devastating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.960 --> 00:27:03.200
lands as they went. As the Prince neared the Loire&nbsp;
River, he began to realise the serious danger his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:11.600
army was in, as Lancaster had ended his Normandy&nbsp;
campaign in the face of a larger French force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:20.400
whilst John II was building an immense army at&nbsp;
Chartres. Moreover, while John remained unpopular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:28.000
with the French aristocracy, they now rallied&nbsp;
to him out of fear of the Black Prince. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:34.640
the king was able to assemble a force more than&nbsp;
twice the size of the Prince’s, who now became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:42.400
desperate to locate and link up with Lancaster’s&nbsp;
army. On the 7th of September, the Prince’s army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:50.640
reached the Loire in the suburbs of Tours, aiming&nbsp;
to join Lancaster who was just 70 miles away on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:59.600
the north bank. However, heavy rains soon made the&nbsp;
swollen river impassable, and after receiving news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.600 --> 00:28:07.760
that John’s army was advancing towards him just&nbsp;
20 miles away, the Black Prince ordered a retreat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.760 --> 00:28:15.840
whilst Lancaster withdrew into Brittany, leaving&nbsp;
the Black Prince to fend for himself. With both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:22.400
armies moving south, the French had managed&nbsp;
to overtake and cut across the English line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:31.040
of march and were now west of their enemy, near&nbsp;
Poitiers. It was clear that a major confrontation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:44.560
was imminent, but while the Prince led about 7,000&nbsp;
men, King John had over 20,000 men with him. 
&nbsp;

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:50.880
On the 18th of September, the Prince took&nbsp;
up his position south-east of Poitiers. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:58.640
forces occupied a ridge of high ground, while his&nbsp;
flanks were protected by thick woods on one side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.640 --> 00:29:05.040
with wagons from the baggage train, used&nbsp;
to shield his flanks further. The Prince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:05.040 --> 00:29:11.120
placed Warwick and Oxford’s division on his&nbsp;
left, Suffolk and Salisbury’s on his right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:11.120 --> 00:29:18.560
whilst he commanded the centre. His longbowmen&nbsp;
were placed on the wings of the army. On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:26.800
morning of the 19th, King John and the French had&nbsp;
arrived. He formed his army into three divisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:32.000
led by the Dauphin Charles, John’s&nbsp;
brother Philip the Duke of Orleans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:39.840
and himself. At 7:30am the Prince gathered his&nbsp;
carts and moved them in a southern direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:47.440
as if he were retreating. The feint worked and&nbsp;
the French cavalry charged the English line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:54.080
This charge met with initial success as the&nbsp;
armoured French horses resisted the Prince’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.080 --> 00:30:01.440
archers and crashed into the Anglo-Gascons.&nbsp;
However, after repositioning to the side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:08.320
the English archers targeted the horses behind the&nbsp;
front line to devastating effect, destroying the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:08.320 --> 00:30:15.280
momentum of the French assault. The cavalry that&nbsp;
had broken through the defences were surrounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:22.000
and cut down. The English then attacked the&nbsp;
exhausted French men-at-arms through the hedges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:29.040
and spilt around the flanks of the French line,&nbsp;
forcing them to retreat after two hours of bloody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:29.040 --> 00:30:37.280
fighting. The strongest French division under King&nbsp;
John now began to advance. While this presented a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.280 --> 00:30:44.960
grave threat, the Black Prince launched a surprise&nbsp;
cavalry charge directly at the French line while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.960 --> 00:30:52.800
also sending a mounted force on a large flanking&nbsp;
manoeuvre to fall on the French left. A melee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.800 --> 00:31:00.640
ensued, with both sides taking casualties. But&nbsp;
then the Gascon reserve force crashed into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.640 --> 00:31:08.960
French left flank, causing widespread panic in&nbsp;
John’s ranks. The English archers, having expended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.960 --> 00:31:16.080
their arrows, threw down their bows and launched&nbsp;
themselves into the battle. With contingents of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:25.200
the French army now fleeing, the French King was&nbsp;
surrounded and captured by the English. 
&nbsp;

00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:34.320
On the evening of the 19th of September,&nbsp;
the French dead stood at 2,500 men-at-arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:41.440
while the king and dozens of notable lords&nbsp;
were captured. From the jaws of defeat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.440 --> 00:31:49.520
the Black Prince had snatched an improbable and&nbsp;
total victory. The captured King John was treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.520 --> 00:31:55.040
respectfully by the Black Prince, who waited&nbsp;
on his table on the evening after the battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:55.040 --> 00:32:03.200
and praised the King’s valour and bravery. If the&nbsp;
central tenets of medieval chivalry were bravery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:11.120
in battle and respect for one’s enemy, then at&nbsp;
Poitiers the Black Prince had demonstrated why he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.120 --> 00:32:18.960
would be remembered in history as the embodiment&nbsp;
of a chivalrous warrior. The Anglo-Gascon army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:26.080
returned to Bordeaux on the 5th of October, to&nbsp;
great public jubilation. Thereafter a two-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:26.080 --> 00:32:35.040
truce with the captive King John was agreed on&nbsp;
the 23rd of March 1357. The Prince and his royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:42.400
prisoner set sail for England and on the 24th&nbsp;
of May Edward of Woodstock rode through London&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:42.400 --> 00:32:52.560
in triumph. This was the pinnacle of his career as&nbsp;
England’s warrior-prince. On the 8th of May 1358,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.560 --> 00:33:01.200
Edward III signed an agreement with John II, in&nbsp;
which John agreed to pay 4 million French crowns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:09.920
for his release and recognised Edward as sovereign&nbsp;
ruler of the large southern province of Aquitaine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:09.920 --> 00:33:18.320
In return Edward would renounce his claim to&nbsp;
the French throne. This treaty constituted a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.320 --> 00:33:27.200
substantial victory for the English.
Following his victory, the Prince fell back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:34.640
into his ever-persistent problem of excessive&nbsp;
spending as he embarked on an ambitious expansion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:43.040
of his Vale Royal residence in Cheshire, an&nbsp;
Abbey begun by his great-grandfather, Edward I.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.040 --> 00:33:49.680
Nevertheless, when the French provisional&nbsp;
government rejected the treaty signed with John,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:55.520
the Black Prince and his father once again&nbsp;
led a large expedition into France on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:55.520 --> 00:34:05.680
28th October 1359, intending to march on Rheims&nbsp;
and have Edward III crowned as King of France.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:05.680 --> 00:34:11.760
However, the army failed to capture the&nbsp;
city and Edward decided to negotiate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:19.120
agreeing to the Treaty of Bretigny on the 8th&nbsp;
of May 1360. Under the terms of the treaty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:19.120 --> 00:34:27.040
Edward ceded lands in the north and west of France&nbsp;
totalling around a third of the country. In return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:34.480
Edward relinquished his claim to the French throne&nbsp;
and lowered John’s ransom to 3 million crowns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:35.120 --> 00:34:43.200
With the war with France now briefly in abeyance,&nbsp;
the 31 year-old Black Prince married Joan of Kent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:43.200 --> 00:34:52.480
his 33-year-old cousin, in the summer of 1361. He&nbsp;
returned to France and soon after he was invested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:59.600
as Prince of Aquitaine as a reward for his victory&nbsp;
at Poitiers. The Prince departed for France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:59.600 --> 00:35:06.960
with most of his household, as well as over 600&nbsp;
soldiers, arriving at Lormont near Bordeaux on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:14.640
29th of June. There the Prince was quite popular&nbsp;
at the beginning of his rule, as his exceedingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:22.640
generous hospitality and grand feasts endeared him&nbsp;
to the local lords. Furthermore, the Prince and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:29.440
his wife were extremely happy in their new domain,&nbsp;
especially so upon the birth of their first child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:38.800
in January 1365, a son whom they named Edward.&nbsp;
In addition, in a meeting of the three estates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:38.800 --> 00:35:45.920
the representative body of Aquitaine, in September&nbsp;
1365, the Prince showed himself to be willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:53.040
listen to the grievances of his subjects, and act&nbsp;
to redress them, working to reduce lawlessness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:53.040 --> 00:36:01.360
in his lands, after hearing complaints of&nbsp;
marauding brigands and feuding lords. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:01.360 --> 00:36:11.840
France had a new king. John II had died in April&nbsp;
1364. The new king, Charles V, proved himself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:11.840 --> 00:36:20.560
be a strong and effective ruler, and a formidable&nbsp;
adversary for the Black Prince, who by 1365 was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:30.000
already experiencing some tension with his nobles,&nbsp;
including his old enemy, the Count of Armagnac.
&nbsp;

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:38.000
The Black Prince’s attention was soon drawn&nbsp;
away from Aquitaine altogether towards Spain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.000 --> 00:36:45.760
when he received a message in 1366 from the&nbsp;
displaced King Pedro of Castile begging the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:45.760 --> 00:36:53.280
Prince to help restore him to the throne. The 32&nbsp;
year-old Pedro had succeeded his father, Alfonso&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:53.280 --> 00:37:01.920
XI, as the sole child of a loveless marriage to&nbsp;
Queen Maria of Portugal, but his father preferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:10.160
his mistress Leonore de Guzman, with whom he had&nbsp;
multiple children who he showered with honour and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.160 --> 00:37:18.240
affection, whilst isolating Pedro, who forged&nbsp;
a strong resentment towards his half-siblings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:27.360
especially the eldest, Enrique of Trastamara. As&nbsp;
King, Pedro was a valuable ally of Edward III,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:36.400
as Castile had emerged as the most powerful state&nbsp;
in the Iberian peninsula. Then, in 1366, Pedro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:44.880
was removed from the throne by his bitter enemy&nbsp;
Enrique, in an invasion supported by Charles V of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:53.200
France. When he fled to Gascony, many of the Black&nbsp;
Prince’s advisors were opposed to helping Pedro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:53.200 --> 00:37:59.600
as in his rapid flight over the Pyrenees&nbsp;
he had not brought any soldiers with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:59.600 --> 00:38:06.240
meaning that the Prince would have to mobilise an&nbsp;
army and bear the financial burden of the campaign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.240 --> 00:38:14.640
if he was to try to restore Pedro to the throne of&nbsp;
Castile. Furthermore, Pedro was widely considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:25.680
to be an unstable tyrant, one who had also been&nbsp;
excommunicated by the Catholic Church. 
	
&nbsp;

00:38:25.680 --> 00:38:33.040
Despite his reservations about Pedro, the English&nbsp;
were wary of allowing Castile to come under French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:40.640
influence and were determined to uphold the&nbsp;
Anglo-Castilian alliance. Therefore, Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:49.600
reluctantly agreed to help restore the Castilian&nbsp;
King on September 23rd 1366, on the condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:49.600 --> 00:38:58.880
that Pedro swear an oath to reform how he ruled if&nbsp;
he was restored to power in Castile, and to also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:58.880 --> 00:39:06.880
repair his relations with the church. After the&nbsp;
birth, on the 6th of January 1367, of the Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:14.320
Prince’s second son, Richard, who would later&nbsp;
become King Richard II of England, the Prince left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.320 --> 00:39:23.280
Bordeaux to join his assembling army of over 6,000&nbsp;
men in the foothills of the Pyrenees. Then, on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.280 --> 00:39:31.280
14th of February 1367, the Prince’s vanguard&nbsp;
under Sir John Chandos, crossed the Pyrenees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:31.280 --> 00:39:38.560
followed on the 20th by the Prince, with the main&nbsp;
body, reaching Pamplona, the capital of Navarre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.560 --> 00:39:46.160
on the 23rd of February. As the Prince’s army&nbsp;
reached Vitoria it suffered under harsh weather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.160 --> 00:39:53.840
and a lack of provisions. While encamped around&nbsp;
Vitoria it was ambushed on the 25th of March by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:53.840 --> 00:40:02.560
a Franco-Castilian force under Enrique, losing&nbsp;
hundreds of men. However, this shock spurred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:02.560 --> 00:40:09.920
the Prince into action, and after retreating&nbsp;
into Navarre he marched south on a new route.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:09.920 --> 00:40:18.400
On the 3rd of April 1367, the two armies would&nbsp;
meet on an open plain a mile east of the town of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.400 --> 00:40:27.200
Najera. Though the exact sizes of the armies are&nbsp;
disputed, it is likely that Enrique of Trastamara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.200 --> 00:40:34.800
held the numerical advantage, but the Black Prince&nbsp;
had the element of surprise. He shielded his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:44.800
advance behind a ridge and appeared unexpectedly&nbsp;
with his army on Enrique’s left flank.
&nbsp;

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:51.040
The surprised Castilians and French launched&nbsp;
a poorly coordinated cavalry charge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:51.040 --> 00:40:58.560
which was shot down by the Anglo-Gascon archers.&nbsp;
Enrique’s Spanish soldiers were not accustomed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:58.560 --> 00:41:04.800
to the devastating effectiveness of the English&nbsp;
longbow, which broke the advancing infantry in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:04.800 --> 00:41:11.120
similar fashion and the Prince, seeing the&nbsp;
enemy’s distress, charged with his mounted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.120 --> 00:41:18.640
soldiers to rout the Spanish and French troops.&nbsp;
The Battle of Najera was another complete victory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:18.640 --> 00:41:25.520
for the Black Prince, whose army inflicted heavy&nbsp;
casualties on the Spanish, whilst sustaining very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:25.520 --> 00:41:33.360
few casualties themselves, much to the delight&nbsp;
of Edward III. Despite this, the Black Prince was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:33.360 --> 00:41:41.920
soon frustrated to learn that Enrique had escaped&nbsp;
to France, to be shielded by Charles V. On the 2nd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:41.920 --> 00:41:48.560
of May at Burgos, Pedro was informed that the&nbsp;
campaign to restore him to his throne had cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:48.560 --> 00:41:58.160
over £400,000 and that the English crown expected&nbsp;
to be reimbursed. Although Edward intended to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:58.160 --> 00:42:05.280
remain with his army in Spain to ensure the debt&nbsp;
was paid, an outbreak of dysentery hit the army in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:05.280 --> 00:42:12.320
August. It was almost certainly at this time that&nbsp;
the Prince himself was struck with the sickness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:12.320 --> 00:42:19.840
from which he would never fully recover. As the&nbsp;
disease spread, the Anglo-Gascons marched back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:19.840 --> 00:42:29.920
across the Pyrenees and returned to Bordeaux in&nbsp;
early September 1367. By autumn of 1367, Pedro’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:29.920 --> 00:42:37.680
failure to repay his debts left the Black Prince&nbsp;
on the verge of bankruptcy, with most of his army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:37.680 --> 00:42:46.000
still unpaid. The Prince was accordingly forced to&nbsp;
resort to taxing Aquitaine, introducing a property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:46.000 --> 00:42:55.680
tax known as the fouage, which was approved by&nbsp;
the estates in January 1368. A cohort of noblemen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:55.680 --> 00:43:02.800
led by the ever-difficult Count of Armagnac,&nbsp;
denounced the tax as oppressive, and refused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:02.800 --> 00:43:12.160
to allow its collection on their lands. 
Following his Spanish expedition, many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.160 --> 00:43:18.480
Prince’s political strengths were fading, as&nbsp;
he now lacked the financial means to maintain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.480 --> 00:43:25.360
loyalty through his generosity and gift giving,&nbsp;
and his personal charm was greatly diminished by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:25.360 --> 00:43:34.480
debilitating sickness, which continually plagued&nbsp;
him from the summer of 1368. In June of that year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:34.480 --> 00:43:41.760
Charles saw his opportunity and took it, entering&nbsp;
an agreement with Armagnac, Albret and the Count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:49.440
of Perigord, three influential Aquitanian nobles&nbsp;
who were resentful of the Prince and his new tax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.440 --> 00:43:56.080
pledging to defend them if the Prince ever&nbsp;
attacked their domains and granted them lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:56.080 --> 00:44:04.640
and funds, whilst the nobles promised to fight for&nbsp;
Charles to recover Aquitaine for the French crown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:13.280
As the Prince’s health worsened, his hold over&nbsp;
Aquitaine became ever more precarious. Edward III,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:13.280 --> 00:44:19.200
whose energy and decisiveness had declined&nbsp;
with age and ill health, failed to provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:19.200 --> 00:44:26.160
the Prince with adequate financial resources,&nbsp;
and had dithered whilst Louis, Duke of Anjou,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:26.160 --> 00:44:32.080
brother of Charles V, began raising troops&nbsp;
in southern France, in coordination with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:32.080 --> 00:44:38.400
rebellious nobles, to threaten the Prince’s&nbsp;
authority. Furthermore, the Prince’s position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:38.400 --> 00:44:46.800
was further weakened when Enrique of Trastamara&nbsp;
reconquered Castile and killed Pedro in March&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:57.120
of 1369, placing a French ally directly south of&nbsp;
the Prince’s lands. In June 1369, following months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:57.120 --> 00:45:04.320
of tension, war broke out once again, between&nbsp;
England and France, and Edward III sent a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:12.240
force under the Earls of Cambridge and Pembroke&nbsp;
to assist the Black Prince. Charles, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:12.240 --> 00:45:19.040
had saved prudently throughout his reign, and had&nbsp;
large funds with which to supply the rebellious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:19.040 --> 00:45:29.840
lords, as towns and castles in vulnerable eastern&nbsp;
Aquitaine began to fall to the French. 
&nbsp;

00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:36.320
As he remained crippled by illness, to add&nbsp;
to the Prince’s woes, he began to lose some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:36.320 --> 00:45:43.920
of his closest military companions. Sir James&nbsp;
Audley died of sickness at the end of August,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:50.720
whilst his most experienced captain in Aquitaine,&nbsp;
Sir John Chandos, was killed in a skirmish in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:50.720 --> 00:45:59.680
Poitou on the 1st of January 1370. The French then&nbsp;
launched an offensive in the summer of 1370 with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:59.680 --> 00:46:07.440
two large armies, one of which, under Charles V’s&nbsp;
brother John, Duke of Berry, captured the city of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:07.440 --> 00:46:15.520
Limoges in north-eastern Aquitaine in mid-August,&nbsp;
after the Bishop Jean de Cros, a trusted friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:15.520 --> 00:46:23.360
of the Black Prince, surrendered it to the French.&nbsp;
The Prince, aghast at the city’s surrender, joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:23.360 --> 00:46:31.760
a 4,000-strong army and marched to Limoges in what&nbsp;
would be his final expedition, though he had to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:39.840
carried in a litter, as he was by now too ill to&nbsp;
mount a horse. Nevertheless, the army arrived and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:39.840 --> 00:46:47.920
laid siege to the city on the 14th of September.&nbsp;
On the 19th, the Prince’s miners succeeded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:47.920 --> 00:46:55.280
in destroying a large section of the city wall,&nbsp;
allowing the English troops to surge into Limoges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:55.280 --> 00:47:03.200
and push back the French defenders. The city was&nbsp;
pillaged and burnt by the retreating French and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:03.200 --> 00:47:09.680
advancing English, and when the Bishop of Limoges&nbsp;
was brought before the Prince, he was told that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:18.880
he deserved execution, although he was ultimately&nbsp;
spared. The recovery of Limoges was an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:18.880 --> 00:47:26.160
victory for the English, but it was clouded by the&nbsp;
news that the Black Prince’s eldest son, Edward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:26.160 --> 00:47:35.200
had died from the plague just days after the fall&nbsp;
of Limoges. This devastating news, combined with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:35.200 --> 00:47:44.560
his own illness, made the Black Prince resolve&nbsp;
to leave Aquitaine and return to England.
&nbsp;

00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:52.720
The capture of Limoges became infamous, as&nbsp;
the French chronicler, Jean Froissart, wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:52.720 --> 00:47:58.720
an account of it in which he claimed that the&nbsp;
Prince’s forces indiscriminately killed a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:58.720 --> 00:48:07.360
part of the civilian population after the city&nbsp;
fell. Froissart’s account was largely fabricated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:07.360 --> 00:48:14.160
While there was substantial property damage,&nbsp;
casualties were limited to around 300 people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:14.160 --> 00:48:20.880
not the 3,000 that Froissart suggested.&nbsp;
Nevertheless, this story of the capture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:20.880 --> 00:48:28.720
of Limoges stained the Prince’s reputation.&nbsp;
In early January 1371, the Prince and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:28.720 --> 00:48:36.320
household sailed to England. He spent much of&nbsp;
the remainder of his life at Berkhamsted Castle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:36.320 --> 00:48:43.520
having left his brother, John of Gaunt, in charge&nbsp;
of the worsening military situation in Aquitaine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:43.520 --> 00:48:52.160
By the time a year’s truce was signed in June&nbsp;
1375 England’s holdings in France had been greatly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:52.160 --> 00:48:59.840
diminished. At home, old King Edward III had&nbsp;
become unpopular, as he came under the influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:59.840 --> 00:49:07.840
of his young mistress, Alice Perrers. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
the Black Prince remained an admired figure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:07.840 --> 00:49:14.080
though his health continued to deteriorate from&nbsp;
the dysentery he had contracted, an illness that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:14.080 --> 00:49:22.880
is easily treated with antibiotics today. As&nbsp;
the Prince approached his 46th year in 1376,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.880 --> 00:49:30.320
he was virtually unable to leave his bed, and on&nbsp;
the 1st of June he was carried to the Palace of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:30.320 --> 00:49:36.160
Westminster so that he could spend the last of&nbsp;
his days with his elderly father and the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:36.160 --> 00:49:46.880
of his family. On Sunday the 8th of June 1376,&nbsp;
Edward of Woodstock said a final prayer and died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:46.880 --> 00:49:52.640
almost certainly from the dysentery that had&nbsp;
plagued him since the Spanish campaign nine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:52.640 --> 00:50:00.480
years earlier. He was then buried in Canterbury&nbsp;
Cathedral, where his tomb can still be seen today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:00.480 --> 00:50:08.880
Just over a year later, his father died, meaning&nbsp;
that the Black Prince’s 10-year old son, Richard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:08.880 --> 00:50:15.600
became England’s first new king in half&nbsp;
a century. Richard II would prove to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:15.600 --> 00:50:23.520
divisive ruler who was deposed in 1399 in what&nbsp;
many historians view as the start of the Wars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:34.320
of the Roses that tore England apart between the&nbsp;
1450s and the ascent of the Tudors in 1485. 
&nbsp;

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:41.120
The Black Prince is regarded as one of the&nbsp;
greatest medieval warriors in the history of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:41.120 --> 00:50:47.680
England, playing a leading role in two of the most&nbsp;
decisive English victories of the Hundred Years’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:47.680 --> 00:50:55.920
War at Crecy and Poitiers. He was respected by&nbsp;
the people of England throughout his life and by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:55.920 --> 00:51:02.960
his friends and soldiers, whom he never ceased&nbsp;
to reward generously for their service to him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:02.960 --> 00:51:11.040
Even the French Kings, John II and Charles V,&nbsp;
recognised the Prince’s valour and honour. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:17.440
Prince’s reputation has significant blemishes&nbsp;
however, especially in France where his brutal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:17.440 --> 00:51:24.880
raids through Languedoc and northern Aquitaine,&nbsp;
prior to the battle of Poitiers included mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:24.880 --> 00:51:31.840
atrocities towards civilians and the destruction&nbsp;
of significant swathes of French countryside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:32.400 --> 00:51:38.480
Nevertheless, the Black Prince was&nbsp;
above all else a hero for his time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:38.480 --> 00:51:44.480
labelled by his surgeon John Arderne,&nbsp;
who accompanied him on many campaigns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:44.480 --> 00:51:51.680
as “the very flower of chivalry”, for there&nbsp;
are few men in history who embodied medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:51.680 --> 00:51:58.400
chivalry as much as Edward of Woodstock did.&nbsp;
The principles of courage in battle and showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:58.400 --> 00:52:04.640
honour towards one’s opponents defined the&nbsp;
warrior-Prince’s life, who lived and died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:04.640 --> 00:52:12.560
adhering to a code of chivalry that was widely&nbsp;
aspired to in medieval Europe. Chivalry in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:12.560 --> 00:52:22.640
ways defined the era of the Black Prince, and the&nbsp;
Black Prince in many ways defined chivalry. 
&nbsp;

00:52:24.240 --> 00:52:31.440
What do you think of the Black Prince? Was he a&nbsp;
ruthless military commander who devastated large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:31.440 --> 00:52:38.880
parts of France through his chevauchée raids,&nbsp;
or would he have made a great English king?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:38.880 --> 00:52:49.040
Please let us know in the comment section and in&nbsp;
the meantime thank you very much for watching.

